Unreleased
---
Bugfix
- DockPanel::minimumSizeHint() asked whether the title bar is visible when it
  meant whether the panel has one, and so left the title bar height out of the
  bound while the window had not been shown yet - isVisible() is false for the
  whole tree until then. Everything else in this library asks that question of
  isHidden() (openedDockPanels(), topLevelDockPanel(), the drop overlay), so
  this was the one place that disagreed with its neighbours. The hint is asked
  again rather than cached, so the value corrects itself once the window is
  shown; what made it worth fixing is that the contradiction was invisible,
  not that the damage lasted. tst_DockPanel::minimumSizeHintKeepsTheTitleBar

Cleanup
- Drop the Qt 5.12.2 branch in DockFloatingContainer::event(). The threshold
  sits below the 5.15 minimum K1 set, so the "#if" side is the one that always
  ran: the workaround for the wrong mouse button that
  QEvent::NonClientAreaMouseButtonPress reports from 5.12.2 on is now
  unconditional, and the "#else" side that checked for the left mouse button is
  gone.

Other
- The X11 helpers no longer include a QPA header. x11_connection() reached the
  X connection through QPlatformNativeInterface and its "connection" key, which
  is Qt's private platform API and needs <qpa/qplatformnativeinterface.h> - a
  header Qt 6.12 does not install, so this library stopped compiling on it. Qt 6
  now asks for QNativeInterface::QX11Application, the documented replacement:
  typed instead of string keyed, and public, so it needs no private header. Qt 5
  keeps the old call, having no QNativeInterface. The connection is the same
  either way; only the way of asking for it changed.

0.2.2
---
Bugfix
- DockWindow left the features it has locked globally uninitialised. Nothing set
  DockWindowPrivate::m_lockedDockWidgetFeatures, and DockWidget::features()
  answers with the widget's own features minus that value, so every dock widget
  that belonged to a window reported a feature set with arbitrary bits removed
  until the application happened to call lockDockWidgetFeaturesGlobally() - whose
  first call could in turn be swallowed by its "already in that state" early
  return. The field now starts at NoDockWidgetFeatures, which is what the
  accessor documents for a window that has never locked anything.
- DockWidget crashed when a widget with DeleteContentOnClose was closed without
  content. hideDockWidget() deleted the content unconditionally, while the class
  elsewhere runs with no content on purpose: takeWidget() is the documented way
  for a caller to take it back, and showDockWidget() rebuilds it from the widget
  factory. Closing such a widget dereferenced a null pointer.
- Pinning an already pinned widget crashed. DockWidget::setAutoHide() and
  DockPanel::setAutoHide() both take DockSideBarNone to mean "no particular
  side was asked for", but only substituted a real side on the path that creates
  the auto hide container. The path that moves an already pinned widget to
  another side bar passed None on, and a container only ever builds a side bar
  for the four sides, so the lookup came back null and was dereferenced. The
  "Pin" entry of the tab context menu reaches it: it calls setAutoHide(true)
  with the default area, and it is offered for a widget that is already pinned.

Cleanup
- Remove three private fields that were maintained but never read: the container's
  cached visible panel count (visibleDockPanelCount() counts on demand), the flag
  recording whether the pending auto hide timer meant show or hide (the timer
  simulates a press on the tab either way, so the answer never reached anything),
  and the floating container's drag start position (dragging is driven by the
  mouse position captured when the drag starts).
- The title bar's context menu asked whether the panel is auto hidden inside the
  branch where that is already known to be false, so one of the two labels it
  offered could never be shown. The menu still reads the same; the dead arm is gone.
- DockAutoHideContainer kept its own copy of "which side bar areas are
  horizontal" next to the one in DockUtils. The two differed only in their
  default, which is unreachable because a container is only ever built for one
  of the four sides - but that was enough for
  DockAutoHideContainerPrivate::isHorizontal() and
  DockAutoHideContainer::orientation() to be free to contradict each other about
  the same area. The copy is gone and both consult DockUtils now.
- DockSideTab::mouseMoveEvent() tested for DockDraggingTab with the code it was
  supposed to run commented out, and the state is not reachable for a side tab
  in the first place: startFloating() is the only writer of that drag state and
  it only ever stores FloatingWidget. A side tab cannot be reordered, so the
  branch is gone.

0.2.1
---
Cleanup
- Remove the Qt version branches below 5.15, which K1 set as the minimum: the
  hand-written qByteArrayToHex/toHexLower helpers that existed for Qt below 5.9, the
  QPixmap::devicePixelRatio fallback, the QString::SkipEmptyParts fallback, the
  QFlags::setFlag fallback and the QFontMetrics::width fallbacks in DockLabel

Bugfix
- DockPanel: do not reopen an auto hide panel while a widget is inserted into
  it, which unpinned the widget again and left an empty panel in the root
  splitter
- QX_DOCK_DEPRECATED_SINCE(major, minor) never answered true for a deprecation
  that had already happened: QX_DOCK_DISABLE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE defaulted to
  QX_DOCK_VERSION, so the deprecation point was compared against the version being
  built - (0,2,0) > (0,2,0) is false - and the answer flipped back to false on
  every minor bump. The default is now a fixed floor, so an API stays declared for
  as long as it is inside its removal buffer (K26), and raising the cutoff is what
  compiles it out. tst_qxdeprecated covers all seven libraries

Test
- cover DockGroup, DockGroupManager and DockSubWindow with tst_DockGroup
  (structural API, group-name validation, and perspective save/load round-trip)

